  "text": "He executed the code and it told him which horse would win the next race. Bill Benter was among the first to crunch massive amounts of data to predict horse racing and bet money on it. The computer would evaluate each horse by its past performance, weather conditions and even how much rest it had prior to the race. And it worked. The model consistently won more than it lost. But it wasn't accurate enough to be profitable after the track took their 20% cut from the bet pool. So he was bleeding money every day. And then he realized that the public collectively was already very good at predicting the winners. Meaning that he wasn't that good, and he wasn't that good, and neither was he. But all of them together, as an average, that was a pretty good predictor. So when he combined his own model with the betting info of the crowd, that was when the model became unstoppable, outperforming even the top expert picks. The model was so good that it predicted a triple trio, guessed the top 3 places out of 3 specific races, and won $20 million. That's 1 in 10 million chance by the way. and his program successfully did it but he didn't even redeem the prize money to avoid shining light on his operation that has reportedly earned him over a billion dollars"
